.TH siglog-decode "1" "2026-11-02" "stratuMAK Documentation" "HAL User's Manual"
.SH NAME
siglog-decode \- decode a HAL signal change log written by the siglog cmod

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B siglog-decode
[\fB\-f\fR \fIFORMAT\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIGLOB\fR]... [\fB\-o\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR] \fIFILE\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B siglog
cmod records every change of the selected HAL signals, tagged with the cycle
number of the thread it runs in, to a binary file for the whole lifetime of
the instance:
.PP
.RS
.nf
load siglog file=siglog-%Y%m%d-%H%M%S.bin include=joint.*
addf siglog.sample servo-thread
.fi
.RE
.PP
(\fBinclude=\fR, \fBexclude=\fR and \fBdepth=\fR are optional; without
\fBinclude=\fR every signal is logged.)
.PP
.B siglog-decode
reads such a file and writes it as text, CSV or a Value Change Dump.
Only real changes are in the file; the first logged cycle, and the first
cycle after a loss, hold a full snapshot of every logged signal.
.PP
A file that was not closed cleanly (no END record, e.g. after a crash or a
full disk) is still decoded up to its last complete record.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format, one of:
.RS
.TP
.B text
One line per change: cycle, time, signal, value (the default).
.TP
.B csv
The same as CSV, with the header row
.BR cycle,time,signal,value .
.TP
.B vcd
Value Change Dump, for viewers such as GTKWave or Surfer.  The timestamp is
cycle times the thread period, so it is monotonic even if the thread
jittered.  Bit signals become wires, floats reals, s32 and u32 32-bit
integers.
.TP
.B info
The header only: file version, thread period, start time, the include and
exclude patterns, and the list of logged signals with their types.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-signal\fR \fIGLOB\fR
Only output signals whose name matches the
.BR fnmatch (3)-style
pattern
.IR GLOB .
May be given several times; a signal matching any of them is output.
Without it, every logged signal is output.  Ignored by
.BR "\-f info" .
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Write to
.I OUTPUT
instead of standard output.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show a usage summary and exit.

.SH OUTPUT
Cycle numbers count calls of the siglog RT function; cycle 0 is the first
call.  Times in
.B text
and
.B csv
are seconds since the first logged cycle.  They follow the real clock: the
file carries a timestamp once per second of run time, and each cycle's time is
derived from the last one, rather than assuming a perfect period.  Changes
before the first timestamp have an empty time.
.PP
Gaps are marked in the output.  In
.B text
they are comment lines:
.TP
.BI "# lost " N " cycle(s) from cycle " C
the ring between the RT function and the writer thread was full, and the
changes of
.I N
cycles were dropped.  The next row is a full snapshot again.
.TP
.B "# end of file without END record (not closed cleanly)"
the file ends early.
.PP
In
.B csv
the same gaps are rows with
.B #lost
(value = number of dropped cycles, starting at that cycle) or
.B #truncated
in the signal column.  In
.B vcd
a loss is a
.B $comment
line.
.PP
Floats are printed with full precision, so \-0.0 and 0.0 are distinct.

.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success.  A file that is not a siglog file, has an unsupported version,
or is truncated inside its header (logging never started) is reported on
standard error with a non-zero exit status.

.SH EXAMPLES
Show what a file contains:
.PP
.RS
.nf
siglog-decode \-f info siglog-20261102-101500.bin
.fi
.RE
.PP
The changes of the joint 0 signals as CSV:
.PP
.RS
.nf
siglog-decode \-f csv \-s 'j0.*' \-o j0.csv siglog-20261102-101500.bin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open everything in a waveform viewer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
siglog-decode \-f vcd \-o run.vcd siglog-20261102-101500.bin
gtkwave run.vcd
.fi
.RE

.SH NOTES
The file format is documented at the top of
.I src/hal/components/siglog.c
in the stratuMAK sources.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR halsampler (1),
.BR halcmd (1)
